DPDK EAL Memory Padding & Channel/Rank Interleaving
==================================================

1) Problem EAL is solving
------------------------

Modern CPUs:
- Have multiple memory channels
- Each channel has limited bandwidth
- Accessing the same channel repeatedly creates contention

DPDK workload:
- Touches first ~64 bytes of many packet buffers
- Happens at very high rate (millions of packets/sec)
- Small inefficiencies = big performance loss

If many buffers start at addresses that map to:
- same channel
- same rank

Then:
- memory controller serializes access
- stalls occur
- lower throughput is observed


2) How CPU decides channel / rank (address bits)
------------------------------------------------

Physical address bits are used by the memory controller to select:
- channel
- DIMM
- rank
- bank

Simplified physical address layout:

| ... | CH bits | RANK bits | BANK bits | OFFSET |

Example mapping:
- bits 6–7   → channel
- bits 8–9   → rank
- bits 10–12 → bank

So addresses map like this:

Address 0x1000 → Channel 0, Rank 0
Address 0x1040 → Channel 1, Rank 0
Address 0x1080 → Channel 2, Rank 0
Address 0x10C0 → Channel 3, Rank 0

Important:
Addresses are INTERLEAVED, not contiguous per channel


3) What happens WITHOUT EAL padding
-----------------------------------

mbuf size = 2048 bytes
mbufs allocated back-to-back

mbuf0 → address A
mbuf1 → address A + 2048
mbuf2 → address A + 4096

If channel bits don’t change:

mbuf0 → Channel 0
mbuf1 → Channel 0
mbuf2 → Channel 0

Result:
All packet headers hit the same channel
→ channel saturation
→ memory bottleneck


4) What EAL does (THIS IS THE KEY)
---------------------------------

EAL knows:
- number of memory channels
- number of ranks
- cache line size

Using this information, EAL:
- adds padding between objects
- intentionally shifts start addresses

Instead of:

mbuf0 @ A
mbuf1 @ A + 2048
mbuf2 @ A + 4096

EAL forces:

mbuf0 @ A            → Channel 0, Rank 0
mbuf1 @ A + padding  → Channel 1, Rank 0
mbuf2 @ A + padding  → Channel 2, Rank 0
mbuf3 @ A + padding  → Channel 3, Rank 1


5) What “padding” actually means
--------------------------------

Padding = unused bytes inserted so that
address bits used for channel and rank selection change

Padding size is chosen so that:
- channel bits flip
- rank bits flip

This is intentional address manipulation, not random


6) Resulting memory access pattern
----------------------------------

Packet burst processing:

Core 0 → mbuf → Channel 0
Core 1 → mbuf → Channel 1
Core 2 → mbuf → Channel 2
Core 3 → mbuf → Channel 3

Now:
- multiple channels are active in parallel
- no serialization occurs
- full memory bandwidth is utilized


7) Why this matters for packet processing
-----------------------------------------

DPDK packet path:
- RX burst
- parse L2/L3/L4 headers
- forwarding decision

Only the first ~64 bytes are accessed heavily

If those 64 bytes are:
- spread across different channels and ranks

Then:
- parallel DRAM access
- lower latency
- higher packets-per-second (PPS)

